## 1. Product behaviour (what the user sees)

1. On any YouTube video card (home feed, search results, sidebar, shorts shelf), clicking the 3-dot menu shows a new item **"Summarize"** placed **between "Save to playlist" and "Download"**, with a sparkle icon, larger than native items (16px, weight 500, ≥44px tall).
2. Clicking it opens a **window** for that video. Windows are *per video* (one window per video id; clicking Summarize again focuses the existing one).
3. A window can be **minimized** to a Gmail-style tab docked bottom-right (title + status icon + separate maximize and close buttons) while it keeps running, so the user can keep scrolling and add more videos. Many windows run **in parallel and independently**, each with its own model, key and options.
4. A docked tab shows a spinner while working, a check when done, a red "!" on failure, and turns **green + bold + pulses** when a job completes while it is minimized (until opened).
5. Everything stays **inside the same YouTube tab**: no new tabs/windows. Timestamps and frame cards play the video in an **in-window embedded player**.
6. Summaries/conversations are **cached**; clicking Summarize on an already-summarized video reopens the saved summary **and follow-up conversation** with **no API request**. The user can **Regenerate** at any time.
7. Open windows are **shared across tabs**: a window opened (docked) in one YouTube tab appears minimized in every other YouTube tab, including newly opened ones, until closed; progress syncs live. Closing in one tab closes it everywhere. Cached content survives closing, so selecting Summarize again resumes the conversation.
8. Each window has a **follow-up question** box pinned at the bottom (stays visible while the body scrolls).
9. Multiple **API keys** (Google AI Studio) with nicknames/descriptions, chosen per window.
10. The summary **excludes sponsor segments and creator self-promotion** (merch, Patreon/memberships/donations, unrelated charities, tour dates, creator's other products/services/courses/channels, "like and subscribe"), and (optionally) includes fact-checking, relevant images/diagrams/frames, and links to external sources.

