## 7. Content pipeline

`produce(win, job)`:

- **Ask model** → `askRun` (§12); summaries only; forces Source=Video.
- Else if `source==='text'`: fetch the YouTube transcript (§11) (with `withRetries` using `opts.retries` in Transcribe mode); in **Transcribe + Transcript** mode return the captions as Markdown (`## Transcript (YouTube captions)` then one `[MM:SS] text` paragraph per segment) with note "Taken straight from YouTube's captions… No AI was involved." — **no API call**.
- Else build the prompt (summary or transcript) and call Gemini through `runPolicy` (§9). Parts for video source: `[{file_data:{file_uri:'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=<id>'}}, {text:prompt}]` (**video part first**); for transcript source: one text part = prompt + `"\n\nTRANSCRIPT:\n"` + `[MM:SS] …` lines.
- If the call fails with 429 whose message/quota ids match `/video|hour|youtube/i` and `opts.txFallback` → switch `win.source='text'`, recurse, and prepend note "The video-input limit was reached, so the transcript was used instead."
- If `opts.gen` and mode is summary → `addGeneratedImages` (§10).
- On success `onDone`: save cache (before commit), render, set unread if minimized/hidden, commit, and if (fc or src) and summary mode → auto-run the fact pass (§8).

**Summary prompt** (product-defining; keep wording intent):
```
Watch this YouTube video (titled "<title>") and write a verbose, faithful summary in English.      [transcript source: "Read this timestamped transcript of a YouTube video …"]
Use Markdown with these sections, in this order (omit a section if it does not apply):
- "## TL;DR": 2-3 sentences capturing the core message.
- "## Detailed summary": walk through the video chronologically. Start each part with a heading "### [MM:SS] Section title" (use [H:MM:SS] after one hour), then cover the specific claims, arguments, examples, steps, names, numbers and conclusions … short paragraphs and bullets. Be thorough rather than terse.
- "## Key takeaways": bulleted list.
- "## Notable details": figures, tools, products, brief quotes and caveats that matter.
- "## Worth your time?": 1-2 sentences on who it is for and whether it delivers on its title or is clickbait / padded.
EXCLUDE entirely: sponsor segments, paid promotions and ad reads; and any self-promotion by the creator or guests – merchandise, Patreon/memberships/donation requests, charities or causes unrelated to the video's subject, tour dates or live events, courses, other products or services they sell, and plugs for their other channels, newsletters or social media (including "like and subscribe" requests). Treat these as if they were not in the video: do not summarize, mention or timestamp them, however much runtime they take up. A cause or organization that is genuinely the subject of the video is not self-promotion.
Only report what is actually said or shown. Never invent details, quotes, timestamps or URLs.
[if opts.gsearch] Use Google Search to verify facts. Only include URLs that appear in your search results; never guess or reconstruct a URL.
[if opts.img] Visuals: add marker lines after the relevant paragraph (max 6): "[[FRAME mm:ss | caption]]" for a moment where the video itself shows a diagram/chart/graph/slide/map/demo (only if you can see it; omitted for transcript source); "[[IMG search terms | caption]]" for a standard illustration to be looked up on Wikimedia Commons (short generic English terms). Nothing else.
[if opts.gen] AI images: "[[GEN image prompt | caption]]" (max 3). You are only DESIGNING the prompt for an image model (Nano Banana) which draws it afterwards; each prompt must be self-contained (subject, layout, clean flat educational style, short English labels, aspect ratio); no real people's likenesses, logos or copyrighted characters.
If the video is mostly music or has little speech, describe what happens instead.
```
Note: there is **no Fact-check / Further-reading section in the main prompt** (moved to the separate pass, §8).

**Transcript prompt (Transcribe, video source)**: "Watch this YouTube video … produce a complete, accurate transcript of everything spoken, in the original language(s). Markdown; new paragraph per speaker/topic change starting with [MM:SS] ([H:MM:SS] after an hour); label speakers only if >1 voice; do not summarize/paraphrase/skip; mention significant non-speech audio in italics in brackets only when it matters; never invent words or timestamps." No tools, no exclusion rule.

**Follow-up prompt**: states what was watched/read and that the user has a follow-up; answer accurately using the video (video source, re-sent as a `file_data` part) and Google Search if `gsearch`; cite `[MM:SS]`; say so if not available; same EXCLUDE rule but "only discuss those if the user asks"; then `SUMMARY YOU WROTE:` (or `TRANSCRIPT YOU WROTE:`) in triple quotes, last `historyTurns` Q/A pairs, `FOLLOW-UP QUESTION:`. Transcript-source follow-ups send **no** video/transcript, only the summary as context. Follow-ups use the same policy (retry 0, optional fallback), save to cache, and their answers render the same markdown/notes/sources.

